be nice in this world and generous.

259
00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:35.220
Yeah, people wanna help.

260
00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:36.376
They wanna help, yeah.

261
00:11:36.376 --> 00:11:37.930
But were you dying of embarrassment inside?

262
00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:39.429
Of course, oh my God I was absolutely

263
00:11:39.429 --> 00:11:42.060
dying of embarrassment and don't forget Rich,

264
00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:46.590
like I wasn't planning on having to audition that day

265
00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:48.220
for a total stranger, right.

266
00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.460
So I was definitely like putting, being put on the spot

267
00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:53.610
and thankfully I was practicing a lot,

268
00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:55.370
so I was relatively prepared for the moment.

269
00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.257
But you brought your violin with you.

270
00:11:57.257 --> 00:12:00.151
Yeah, I mean, I was playing for another,

271
00:12:00.151 --> 00:12:01.817
another audition that day.

272
00:12:01.817 --> 00:12:06.460
And so I had it with me and, but I wasn't, you know,

273
00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:08.530
prepared to play for a Julliard teacher

274
00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.037
with a new piece that day.

275
00:12:10.037 --> 00:12:12.205
You're you're nine, right?

276
00:12:12.205 --> 00:12:14.979
Yeah, I was nine exactly.

277
00:12:14.979 --> 00:12:17.640
And, but yet I think I inherited

278
00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:19.650
some of my mom's fearlessness about the whole thing.

279
00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:21.560
So while I was totally embarrassed

280
00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:23.662
about the walking to Juilliard approach,

281
00:12:23.662 --> 00:12:26.750
clearly I had some of that fearlessness in me

282
00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:28.260
because when I was given the opportunity

283
00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:30.720
to play for the teacher, I wasn't running out of the room.

284
00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.270
I was firmly planting my feet in the ground,

285
00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.044
thinking, this is my moment.

286
00:12:35.044 --> 00:12:36.850
Like, this is the moment it's come.

287
00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:38.570
Finally, and I have to seize it.

288
00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:42.973
And so I played for him on the spot and he accepted me

289
00:12:42.973 --> 00:12:45.262
into a summer program that ended up changing my life

290
00:12:45.262 --> 00:12:48.105
because it was a summer music festival

291
00:12:48.105 --> 00:12:50.620
bootcamp of sorts for me.

292
00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:55.407
And, you know, five or of weeks he brought me from,

293
00:12:55.407 --> 00:12:57.735
and he said this after the fact that when he first heard me,

294
00:12:57.735 --> 00:13:00.130
he thought I had no chance.

295
00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:00.980
Of getting into Juilliard.

296
00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:03.228
I think he literally said he just liked my personality.

297
00:13:03.228 --> 00:13:05.930
So I was like the personality card.

298
00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:09.130
Right, I mean, you have a lot of humility around this.

299
00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:12.160
Like you always, you know, push back on the notion

300
00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:13.733
that you're some kind of prodigy, right.

301
00:13:13.733 --> 00:13:17.750
Yeah, I truly do not believe I was prestigious.

302
00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:20.569
And I don't say that with any sort of false humility

303
00:13:20.569 --> 00:13:24.220
it's that I was actually exposed to real prodigy.

304
00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:29.220
So I saw what actually defines a prodigy and I wasn't there,

305
00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:31.600
but I'm fine with it.

306
00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:34.563
Like, I think that there were certain,

307
00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.960
there are certain advantages almost to being,

308
00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:40.470
to having limits on your talent, which is that

309
00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:43.350
you spend more of your life just living normal life

310
00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:46.100
and having a diverse set of emotions

311
00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.500
that you can bring into your music.

312
00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:50.800
And I think while, you know, so, you know,

313
00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:52.450
I had this bootcamp with this teacher who eventually

314
00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:54.560
got me to the point where I did audition

315
00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.280
for Juilliard and I was accepted, but my technique

316
00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:01.360
trailed behind my musicality for the entirety of my career.

317
00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:02.491
And I think that might be,

318
00:14:02.491 --> 00:14:04.920
but I think there might have been an advantage to that,

319
00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:06.430
which is, I think maybe something

320
00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:08.110
that we're getting at together here,

321
00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:11.815
which is that when you have limits on your technical

322
00:14:11.815 --> 00:14:15.225
abilities or your raw talent in certain domains,

323
00:14:15.225 --> 00:14:17.270
your attention goes else swear.

324
00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:20.130
And for me it was, well, I think my goal

325
00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:21.893
is just to produce beautiful music.

326
00:14:21.893 --> 00:14:23.609
And it just became, that sort of a.

327
00:14:23.609 --> 00:14:25.726
In that regard, you're not held captive by

328
00:14:25.726 --> 00:14:29.317
this incredible talent that then drives your life.

329
00:14:29.317 --> 00:14:34.317
I know in my own life, you know, I've entered realms where

330
00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:36.880
I certainly was nowhere near the most talented,

331
00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:40.150
but I also had a work capacity to kind of narrow

332
00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:41.580
that talent deficit gap.

333
00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:43.915
And my sense is that when, you know,

334
00:14:43.915 --> 00:14:46.250
you're not as talented as these other people,

335
00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:49.170
you have to shoulder the responsibility of doing the work.

336
00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.538
Whereas people who are brimming with talent

337
00:14:51.538 --> 00:14:54.410
have a different relationship with process

338
00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:57.180
because they can always fall back on the talent.

339
00:14:57.180 --> 00:15:00.160
Yeah, I think, I mean, that definitely resonates with me

340
00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:04.110
in other areas of my life, but I'm not sure

341
00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:06.620
that it applied in the music space and I'll tell you why,

342
00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:10.100
which is I was not out working my peers.

343
00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:13.030
So that was something that was pretty defining in terms of

344
00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:16.390
what my childhood was like versus the classmates that I had.

345
00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:20.210
I had classmates, I remember one in particular

346
00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:22.850
Shunsuke Sato, who is now a professional violinist

347
00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:24.190
who lives in France or something.

348
00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:26.120
And he was practicing his age equivalent.

349
00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:29.000
And I know this because we were neighbors at summer camp.

350
00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:31.710
So I was next to him, his apartment.

351
00:15:31.710 --> 00:15:34.202
And I could hear him practicing eight hours a day,

352
00:15:34.202 --> 00:15:36.671
nine hours a day, 10 hours a day.

353
00:15:36.671 --> 00:15:41.671
And my mom was kind of like a couple hours, dude, that's it.

354
00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:44.400
That's our max here in the Shankar family.

355
00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:47.374
I want you having other hobbies.

356
00:15:47.374 --> 00:15:50.167
You know, I played soccer when I was in elementary school.

357
00:15:50.167 --> 00:15:54.210
I did art classes, tried out for the school plays.

358
00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:56.070
Like she was really intent

359
00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.170
that I have some kind of balance in my life.

360
00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:00.793
And so I think that,

361
00:16:02.354 --> 00:16:04.940
but again, I think that there are various ways

362
00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:08.430
that that balance can manifest, right.

363
00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:12.070
It did make me the least technically strong player

364
00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:13.430
in my peer group.

365
00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:16.696
But I think it made me one of the stronger

366
00:16:16.696 --> 00:16:19.500
musical members of my peer group.

367
00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:21.060
Because I do feel like I had just

368
00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:23.617
more to call upon in life to bring to my music.

369
00:16:23.617 --> 00:16:25.980
You you're living a richer life experience.

370
00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:28.392
So you have more, you have more to bring

371
00:16:28.392 --> 00:16:32.760
to the music to personalize it and add your flare

372
00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:34.450
because you're actually living your life

373
00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:37.905
and not just sitting in a room practicing.

374
00:16:37.905 --> 00:16:40.490
Yeah, which is, I mean, it's such a lonely sport,

375
00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:42.248
you know I mean, I think that's the one thing

376
00:16:42.248 --> 00:16:44.022
I don't miss about my violin years.

377
00:16:44.022 --> 00:16:48.845
Which is a very lonely enterprise when you're a soloist

378
00:16:48.845 --> 00:16:53.128
that's it's you and the bow and the violin

379
00:16:53.128 --> 00:16:54.260
in a room for hours.

380
00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:56.710
Pure about that too, I guess.

381
00:16:56.710 --> 00:16:58.750
But I didn't even know that I mean,

382
00:16:58.750 --> 00:17:01.490
I've always thought of Juilliard as this conservancy,

383
00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:04.370
that that is sort of a collegiate experience.

384
00:17:04.370 --> 00:17:05.950
I didn't even know that they had programs

385
00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:08.420
outside of that for younger people.

386
00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:10.086
So yeah, there's a pre-college division.

387
00:17:10.086 --> 00:17:11.230
Right, so that's what you were in.

388
00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:13.750
Was that every day, I mean, you're living in Connecticut.

389
00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:15.540
You couldn't have been going down to Manhattan everyday.

390
00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:18.160
No, exactly so the pre-college program demands

391
00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:21.080
that kids are enrolled in some sort of full-time school

392
00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:23.843
outside of it, for some kids it was homeschool.

393
00:17:23.843 --> 00:17:25.500
'Cause they took the violin so seriously.

394
00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:27.960
So they would, I mean, I saw families torn apart

395
00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:31.626
over these musical ambitions, right.

396
00:17:31.626 --> 00:17:35.660
Two people are living in a studio apartment in a family

397
00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:38.732
in Manhattan and then the other two are based in soul

398
00:17:38.732 --> 00:17:41.410
living all the way across the world, right.

399
00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:43.336
To make things work and ends meet.

400
00:17:43.336 --> 00:17:48.030
And so I was in just our public elementary school,

401
00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:50.140
middle school, high school throughout.

402
00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:52.868
And then Saturday I would have to wake up at 4:30

403
00:17:52.868 --> 00:17:55.020
in the morning, my mom and I would take

404
00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:57.844
the Metro north train from Connecticut to New York.

405
00:17:57.844 --> 00:18:00.910
Again, she says wild to her.

406
00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:02.470
She wakes me at 4:30 in the morning.

407
00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:04.010
She's expecting me to complain.

408
00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:05.227
And instead I jump out of the bed.

409
00:18:05.227 --> 00:18:08.494
You know, and again, I did not show that enthusiasm

410
00:18:08.494 --> 00:18:11.360
for other things so this is definitely special.

411
00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.930
And then I would participate in up to 10 hours of lessons

412
00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:18.500
and classes the whole day and come home super late at night.

413
00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:19.462
That was my whole Saturday.

414
00:18:19.462 --> 00:18:22.838
And then when I was a teenager, when I was 13

415
00:18:22.838 --> 00:18:25.500
and its Perlman asked me

416
00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.120
to be his private violin student, things really picked up.

417
00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:30.220
And now I was traveling to New York sometimes

418
00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:32.760
multiple times a week for private lessons with him,

419
00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:36.123
for studio classes, he was teaching me chamber music,

420
00:18:36.123 --> 00:18:40.006
like everything got, I already thought it was amped up.

421
00:18:40.006 --> 00:18:42.950
And then the dial just went way up at that point.

422
00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:44.980
I mean, that's so crazy.

423
00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:46.960
Explain for people who don't know

424
00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:48.832
like who he is and what a legend he is.

425
00:18:48.832 --> 00:18:53.065
Yeah, I mean, arguably the best,

426
00:18:53.065 --> 00:18:55.040
the best violin player of all time.

427
00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:57.227
And what did he see in you?

428
00:18:57.227 --> 00:19:00.740
You're so humble about your, you know, skill and talent.

429
00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:02.750
Like why did he choose you for this?

430
00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:04.920
Yeah, I mean, it was an interesting situation

431
00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:08.310
where my mom had asked our joint teacher.

432
00:19:08.310 --> 00:19:10.140
So interestingly, Dorothy DeLay

433
00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:11.960
was one of my violin teachers at the time

434
00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:13.143
who had also taught Perlman.

435
00:19:13.143 --> 00:19:15.838
So I was studying with her and my mom being

436
00:19:15.838 --> 00:19:18.630
the go-getter she is, was like, hey, do you think, you know,

437
00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:21.390
at some point Maya can just play one time for Perlman

438
00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:22.660
almost as a fun life experience.

439
00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:25.467
She's got some serious mama bear energy.

440
00:19:25.467 --> 00:19:26.630
She really does.

441
00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:29.580
And, but never was tiger mommy.

442
00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:31.933
So that's an, that's the thing, right.

443
00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:35.380
Which is sometimes you see those two traits coupled,

444
00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:37.980
but she was both like fearless, but then also let us kids

445
00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:41.340
have the freedom of choice in terms of what things

446
00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:43.040
we cared about and wanted to do.

447
00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:45.440
She never pushed us to do things we didn't wanna do, right.

448
00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:47.580
Didn't have that unhealthy relationship

449
00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:48.413
where she's living.

450
00:19:48.413 --> 00:19:50.600
Not at all seriously through your experience.

451
00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:51.440
Yeah, definitely not.

452
00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:56.440
I mean, I do know though that part of her ambition in

453
00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:59.379
exposing us for kids to as much as she did,

454
00:19:59.379 --> 00:20:01.140
I have two older brothers and older sister,

455
00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:03.730
is that growing up in India, you know,

456
00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:05.720
she was encouraged to do well in school

457
00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:08.100
and she occasionally would do Indian classical singing.

458
00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.830
But outside of that, she was mostly in the domain

459
00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:13.860
of domesticated duties.

460
00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:16.508
And I think she really wanted especially for her girls

461
00:20:16.508 --> 00:20:20.213
to have a richer palette of things from which to choose.

462
00:20:20.213 --> 00:20:22.580
And your parents, it was an arranged marriage, right.

463
00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:24.781
Like they only knew each other like 20 days or something.

464
00:20:24.781 --> 00:20:28.283
Yeah, they met on January 1st

465
00:20:28.283 --> 00:20:31.850
and they got married on January 21st.

466
00:20:31.850 --> 00:20:33.300
So they had a choice.

467
00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:35.787
It was an arranged meeting of sorts,

468
00:20:35.787 --> 00:20:38.559
but they both had to decide on January 1st,

469
00:20:38.559 --> 00:20:41.800
the same day they met, if they'd like to get engaged.

470
00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:44.670
And I trace back,

471
00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.210
I mean, some of my mom's fearlessness is just in her jeans,

472
00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:49.570
but she also was put into sort of a

473
00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:50.780
training bootcamp of sorts.

474
00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:53.560
When, you know, she marries this guy that she doesn't know,

475
00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.502
20 days later, she's a fifth grade teacher at the time,

476
00:20:56.502 --> 00:20:58.740
ends up uprooting her entire life

477
00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:01.415
and moves to the United States with my dad

478
00:21:01.415 --> 00:21:05.767
into a little dorm room in Massachusetts.

479
00:21:05.767 --> 00:21:06.600
And doing his postdoc or whatever.

480
00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:09.100
He's doing his postdoc in physics.

481
00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:13.050
And she is, she doesn't know a single person

482
00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:15.477
in this country, you know, she's 21

483
00:21:15.477 --> 00:21:19.549
and has to navigate this new life that she has.

484
00:21:19.549 --> 00:21:23.310
And she said part of her defense mechanisms

485
00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:24.480
were to create a little army around her.

486
00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:26.330
That's one of the reasons she had four kids

487
00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:27.807
was to create a set, you know, she was so used to,

488
00:21:27.807 --> 00:21:30.390
she has 51 first cousins.

489
00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:32.790
So she's coming from this extremely

490
00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:35.204
vibrant social community.

491
00:21:35.204 --> 00:21:38.800
And then she is now living in a small little dorm

492
00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:39.847
with just my dad.

493
00:21:39.847 --> 00:21:43.548
And so that was part of her way of filling the void.

494
00:21:43.548 --> 00:21:45.923
But no, so I, so I think my mom,

495
00:21:46.801 --> 00:21:50.181
I think she was filled with a lot of optimism

496
00:21:50.181 --> 00:21:54.887
that the United States offered so much promise for her

497
00:21:54.887 --> 00:21:56.560
and she was gonna lean the F in.

498
00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:58.740
And that's what she did yeah.

499
00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:00.657
With herself and, and all of us.

500
00:22:00.657 --> 00:22:01.750
Yeah, so, okay.

501
00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:04.105
So you're with Perlman.

502
00:22:04.105 --> 00:22:07.640
What is that experience like and what do you end up learning

503
00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:11.610
from him beyond just, you know, the technique of the violin?

504
00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:15.900
Yeah, so I go and play for him

505
00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:18.709
and I think it's a one time thing and I am not happy

506
00:22:18.709 --> 00:22:20.360
with the way that I've done.

507
00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:23.170
So I'm just like also that was around the time that I was

508
00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:26.012
getting interested in like MTV and TRL and Britney Spears.

509
00:22:26.012 --> 00:22:29.224
And my brain was a little conflicted about whether,

510
00:22:29.224 --> 00:22:30.950
or this classical music thing was for me,

511
00:22:30.950 --> 00:22:33.130
that was my version of teenage rebellious.

512
00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:33.963
How dare you?

513
00:22:33.963 --> 00:22:35.499
I know how dare I am, and so I don't know.

514
00:22:35.499 --> 00:22:37.699
I don't know if I was really practicing my,

515
00:22:37.699 --> 00:22:39.562
at my peak when it mattered most.

516
00:22:39.562 --> 00:22:41.320
Meanwhile I'm sure you're getting A pluses

517
00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:42.235
and everything in school, right.

518
00:22:42.235 --> 00:22:43.068
(Shankar laughing)

519
00:22:43.068 --> 00:22:46.196
No, I definitely wasn't, but I remember

520
00:22:46.196 --> 00:22:49.900
I played for him and again was not expecting much.

521
00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:54.500
And then he told our joint teacher, he said,

522
00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:57.456
I like to take Maya on as a private student.

523
00:22:57.456 --> 00:22:59.530
And that just knocked my socks off.

524
00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:01.810
I mean, he had a handful of students at the time,

525
00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.099
probably had four students at the time.

526
00:23:04.099 --> 00:23:07.215
I just could not believe that I was one of them.

527
00:23:07.215 --> 00:23:08.643
I mean, I was truly astonished.

528
00:23:08.643 --> 00:23:11.198
What was that about, like, what did he see in you?

529
00:23:11.198 --> 00:23:15.930
I asked his wife candidly later, you know,

530
00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:18.930
because I wanted to know we met up for coffee

531
00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:21.540
just a few years ago in California and I was like,

532
00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:26.030
why did Itzhak, take me on out of all the genius kids

533
00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:27.240
that were like running around.

534
00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:29.479
'Cause I know that technically I was not that strong.

535
00:23:29.479 --> 00:23:34.365
And she said, because he felt you had something to say.

536
00:23:34.365 --> 00:23:37.080
Kind of hits on what we were talking about, right.

537
00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:40.706
I think we had some sort of emotional connection

538
00:23:40.706 --> 00:23:44.292
just in our interactions and the way that we were,

539
00:23:44.292 --> 00:23:46.960
what I was saying to him, through my music,

540
00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:48.600
for whatever reason touched him.

541
00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:51.390
Which is looking back actually like the highest praise

542
00:23:51.390 --> 00:23:52.575
I could have gotten at his musician.

543
00:23:52.575 --> 00:23:54.768
I don't think I internalized it at the time,

544
00:23:54.768 --> 00:23:58.300
because again, I was at all these insecurities

545
00:23:58.300 --> 00:23:59.133
but everything else.

546
00:23:59.133 --> 00:24:02.020
But I think the thing that Perlman,

547
00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:04.401
you know, Perlman is a performer.

548
00:24:04.401 --> 00:24:07.750
He is not, he's not trained in pedagogy, right.

549
00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:09.217
He wasn't trained to be a violin teacher.

550
00:24:09.217 --> 00:24:11.617
And so it's very interesting to see

551
00:24:11.617 --> 00:24:15.090
that translation from like star violinist to teacher.

552
00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:17.116
I had no idea what his approach was gonna be.

553
00:24:17.116 --> 00:24:20.830
And one thing that was so interesting about his approach

554
00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:23.294
is that he didn't do a lot of handholding,

555
00:24:23.294 --> 00:24:27.077
a lot of his work while was trying to teach me

556
00:24:27.077 --> 00:24:29.290
how to teach myself.

557
00:24:29.290 --> 00:24:30.843
Which is really maddening in the moment.

558
00:24:30.843 --> 00:24:33.882
'Cause you're thinking like, dude, you're the genius.

559
00:24:33.882 --> 00:24:36.570
Just tell me how to do fix it's this phrase,

560
00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:38.533
tell me how to make this better.

561
00:24:38.533 --> 00:24:42.093
And instead the lessons were filled

562
00:24:42.093 --> 00:24:43.989
with lines of questioning.

563
00:24:43.989 --> 00:24:45.705
Well, what do you wanna do with this phrase Maya?

564
00:24:45.705 --> 00:24:47.296
How do you want this narrative art to sound?

565
00:24:47.296 --> 00:24:50.027
I'm like, how do you want this narrative art to sound?

566
00:24:50.027 --> 00:24:52.020
You know, you get so frustrated.

567
00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.050
Twisting it into some kind of Zen Cohen,

568
00:24:54.050 --> 00:24:55.460
but how empowering.

569
00:24:55.460 --> 00:24:57.932
It is empowering and you know, if you think about it,

570
00:24:57.932 --> 00:25:02.169
it's so pragmatic because a musician is spending

571
00:25:02.169 --> 00:25:05.864
99% of their time alone in a practice room.

572
00:25:05.864 --> 00:25:09.800
So if you can teach the student to be their own coach,

573
00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:12.000
to be their own teacher.

574
00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:15.600
Now you've just increased your return on investment

575
00:25:16.603 --> 00:25:19.500
a thousand fold, you know, because I'm actually able

576
00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:21.939
to translate that into the way that I approach my practice.

577
00:25:21.939 --> 00:25:24.910
Beyond that though on that subject of having

578
00:25:24.910 --> 00:25:29.000
something to say, I mean ultimately what to distinguishes

579
00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.490
very good musicians from the greatest

580
00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:33.970
is those who have something to say,

581
00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:36.100
it's not the most technically skilled.

582
00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:39.000
It's the people who figure out how to inject it

583
00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:43.360
with their soul or their message or something that is unique

584
00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:47.033
to them that elevates it beyond notes on a page.

585
00:25:47.902 --> 00:25:49.750
No, I think that's right.

586
00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:54.389
And I only had my experience with music.

587
00:25:54.389 --> 00:25:55.880
And you're 15.

588
00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:57.134
Yeah.

589
00:25:57.134 --> 00:25:59.610
Like, how much did you actually have to say at 15?

590
00:25:59.610 --> 00:26:00.641
No, exactly.

591
00:26:00.641 --> 00:26:03.475
But the idea of identifying that potential,

592
00:26:03.475 --> 00:26:06.481
like this is somebody who has something to say

593
00:26:06.481 --> 00:26:09.605
and will continue to have, will continue to develop

594
00:26:09.605 --> 00:26:12.692
a capacity of that expression.

595
00:26:12.692 --> 00:26:14.890
Yeah, and you know, thinking about it also rich,

596
00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:18.866
I think there was, I think there was a lightness of spirit

597
00:26:18.866 --> 00:26:22.946
that I brought to lessons that might have been unusual

598
00:26:22.946 --> 00:26:26.070
because certainly I was in a pressure cooker

599
00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:30.020
but it wasn't for me, it wasn't the end all be all

600
00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:32.020
in the way that it was for other kids and their families

601
00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:34.550
who had literally sacrificed everything

602
00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:36.840
to be within these walls.

603
00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:39.519
And so I think in addition to Perlman

604
00:26:39.519 --> 00:26:43.970
connecting with me musically, I think the fact

605
00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:45.508
we were able to like joke in lessons

606
00:26:45.508 --> 00:26:48.903
and I approached everything with a bit of a reverence

607
00:26:48.903 --> 00:26:52.750
and he was not too holy for me to jab

608
00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:54.491
that and make fun of like, I just,

609
00:26:54.491 --> 00:26:57.110
I think that stuff matters too, right.

610
00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:59.370
Like, do I enjoy my interactions with this person.

611
00:26:59.370 --> 00:27:00.410
What a gift, so it wasn't

612
00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:02.890
like some kind of black Swan situation.

613
00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:04.020
Yeah, no not at all.

614
00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:06.210
And I think looking back,

615
00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:09.700
I just always had rapport matters to me.

616
00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:11.220
Like it took me a long time to realize like,

617
00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:13.860
ah, I'm obsessed with human beings and human connection

618
00:27:13.860 --> 00:27:15.840
and emotional connection and whatnot.

619
00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:18.810
But I felt like I cared much more about the bond

620
00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:21.243
I was developing with any person I was learning from

621
00:27:21.243 --> 00:27:23.670
than I was even about how much better

622
00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:24.610
I became at the violin.

623
00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:26.513
It was so much more about the imediacy

624
00:27:26.513 --> 00:27:28.210
of that personal connection.

625
00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:29.890
And maybe that's part of what he sensed,

626
00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:32.824
which is, this is like a curious person.

627
00:27:32.824 --> 00:27:35.486
Sometimes it translates into the violin, which is great,

628
00:27:35.486 --> 00:27:38.910
but independently it's just fun to have someone

629
00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:41.320
who's curious and that I have a rapport with.

630
00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:43.600
Right, but the real nugget of wisdom in that,

631
00:27:43.600 --> 00:27:46.748
and we're gonna our way up to it is this idea that,

632
00:27:46.748 --> 00:27:51.340
you know, the passion doesn't rest with the vehicle,

633
00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:53.810
for the passion, you have to look beneath the surface

634
00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:55.090
to find what's driving it.

635
00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:57.970
So in your case, you had to learn the hard way

636
00:27:57.970 --> 00:27:59.980
that it wasn't necessarily the violin

637
00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:01.301
that you were passionate about.

638
00:28:01.301 --> 00:28:04.974
It was what the violin allowed you to connect with.

639
00:28:04.974 --> 00:28:07.010
Right, is that a fair way of describing that?

640
00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:09.584
Yeah, absolutely so I mean,

641
00:28:09.584 --> 00:28:12.100
I should learn this lesson the hard way,

642
00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:16.240
which is against my own desires and will

643
00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:17.600
I had to stop playing the violin.

644
00:28:17.600 --> 00:28:20.960
So started studying with Perlman when I was 13.

645
00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:24.450
And when I was 15, I attended his summer music program

646
00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:27.160
on shelter island in New York.

647
00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:29.830
And I remember it was a cold morning thing in July

648
00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:32.536
and I overstretched my finger on a single note

649
00:28:32.536 --> 00:28:36.386
and I heard a popping sound and I knew something bad

650
00:28:36.386 --> 00:28:38.530
had happened, but I kind of was in denial.

651
00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:41.970
And I was also the recalcitrant inpatient teenager.

652
00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:43.730
That was like, I'm gonna will my way, you know,

653
00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:46.000
I'm gonna work my way through this horrible injury.

654
00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:48.770
And of course I didn't.

655
00:28:48.770 --> 00:28:51.060
And even though I tried to play through pain for months

656
00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:55.010
and months on end and just fight my way through,

657
00:28:55.010 --> 00:28:57.370
eventually doctors told me I could never play again

658
00:28:57.370 --> 00:28:58.890
and I just had to listen.

659
00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:00.513
And so when that happened,

660
00:29:01.653 --> 00:29:04.320
I mean, I was just shattered and shell shocked

661
00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:09.320
and then at a loss and I, like you said, you're 15, right.

662
00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:14.020
And so you're not always, so maybe some people

663
00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:16.140
who are really precocious are like this when they're young

664
00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:17.720
and like are very philosophically minded.

665
00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:21.110
But for me, this was a thing that I loved doing.

666
00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:23.920
And I wasn't fully reflective about the role

667
00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:25.280
that it played in my life.

668
00:29:25.280 --> 00:29:27.860
I can make those reflections now after the fact,

669
00:29:27.860 --> 00:29:29.668
but at the time it was just like,

670
00:29:29.668 --> 00:29:32.170
I am the violin, I played the violin, that's it.

671
00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.170
Right, and you were gonna go to conservancy

672
00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:37.010
and this was the life plan and you'd invested a lot.

673
00:29:37.010 --> 00:29:39.967
And suddenly the rug gets pulled out from underneath you.

674
00:29:39.967 --> 00:29:43.390
On some though very few 15 year olds

675
00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:45.400
have a sense of what they want do with their life.

676
00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:49.760
But when you're in that situation and your dream is

677
00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:52.003
eradicated overnight, it wasn't a progressive injury, right.

678
00:29:52.003 --> 00:29:53.265
It was just a one time thing.

679
00:29:53.265 --> 00:29:54.468
No it wasn't, it was a sudden thing.

680
00:29:54.468 --> 00:29:57.400
And you've got a, you're you have to sit with that

681
00:29:57.400 --> 00:29:59.031
and figure out what you're gonna do now.

682
00:29:59.031 --> 00:30:02.930
Yeah I mean, even convincing my parents that conservatory

683
00:30:02.930 --> 00:30:04.790
was an option was a big deal because I think

684
00:30:04.790 --> 00:30:07.040
for the longest time they were thinking let's do

685
00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:08.540
the liberal arts education please,

686
00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:10.459
you know, a lot more security.

687
00:30:10.459 --> 00:30:15.090
And also for both my parents who had had a college

688
00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:17.810
experience, that was very much on the one track in India

689
00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:19.610
at the time you choose your major at the outset.

690
00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:20.835
And that's what you study.

691
00:30:20.835 --> 00:30:25.835
My mom was so enamored by the American college system

692
00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:28.880
where you could take Chinese literature classes

693
00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:30.777
and then you could also take a class on Hinduism

694
00:30:30.777 --> 00:30:32.110
and then you could take a math class.

695
00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:33.610
Like she was just so excited by that.

696
00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:35.110
And so, and they're.

697
00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:36.520
Both working at Yale, right.

698
00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:37.600
Like he's a professor there

699
00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:39.806
and she was working in like student relations.

700
00:30:39.806 --> 00:30:44.230
Yeah, exactly now she helps students get green cards

701
00:30:44.230 --> 00:30:45.810
to study in the U.S.

702
00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:48.734
So she was so excited by the idea of having us go

703
00:30:48.734 --> 00:30:51.410
to liberal arts schools and my three older siblings had,

704
00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:54.148
but I think when Perlman took me on that was the first

705
00:30:54.148 --> 00:30:56.790
moment where I felt my parents were like,

706
00:30:56.790 --> 00:30:58.110
okay, yeah, maybe it.

707
00:30:58.110 --> 00:30:59.540
Maybe we can do the conservatory thing.

708
00:30:59.540 --> 00:31:01.315
It wasn't getting into Julliard at nine.

709
00:31:01.315 --> 00:31:04.513
Oh no, because again, to my point about the fact

710
00:31:04.513 --> 00:31:06.370
that I absolutely was not a prodigy,

711
00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:09.182
like you would need to see the kind of talent

712
00:31:09.182 --> 00:31:10.970
within those walls.

713
00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:14.360
It is remarkable how amazing these kids are.

714
00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:16.800
And so in the same way that, you know,

715
00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:19.239
when you're in sports at those elite levels,

716
00:31:19.239 --> 00:31:22.079
there's just so few spots for people.

717
00:31:22.079 --> 00:31:25.160
The same is true in music, right.

718
00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:26.948
It was just gonna be so hard

719
00:31:26.948 --> 00:31:29.970
to actually to actually make it.

720
00:31:29.970 --> 00:31:32.438
And so, but yeah, I think the conservatory

721
00:31:32.438 --> 00:31:36.019
path was definitely in the carts, you know.

722
00:31:36.019 --> 00:31:38.500
But here we are a slight change of plans.

723
00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:40.190
Yeah, then I had my slight change of plans

724
00:31:40.190 --> 00:31:42.900
and I remember thinking, I expected to mourn the

725
00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:45.556
loss of the instrument, that's natural.

726
00:31:45.556 --> 00:31:48.684
I did not anticipate what it would do to my self structure.

727
00:31:48.684 --> 00:31:53.550
Like I did not in anticipate what a profound loss

728
00:31:53.550 --> 00:31:55.094
of identity I would feel,

729
00:31:55.094 --> 00:31:57.630
truly like the rugs pulled out from underneath you.

730
00:31:57.630 --> 00:31:59.592
And the thing that you've defined yourself by,

731
00:31:59.592 --> 00:32:01.720
for so long is no longer existent.

732
00:32:01.720 --> 00:32:06.450
I mean, to this day my right shoulder slightly elevated

733
00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.648
compared to my left because of all the hours I spent

734
00:32:08.648 --> 00:32:11.438
playing the violin and my spine is slightly curved.

735
00:32:11.438 --> 00:32:14.420
My body literally grew around the ergonomics

736
00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:15.253
of the instrument.

737
00:32:15.253 --> 00:32:17.160
So it was a part of me today still, right.

738
00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:18.190
You know, people talk about like,

739
00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:19.523
oh, back in the day, oh, my Blackberry

740
00:32:19.523 --> 00:32:21.160
is like attached my hands.

741
00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:23.440
Like literally my body grew around this thing

742
00:32:23.440 --> 00:32:24.621
and now I don't that anymore.

743
00:32:24.621 --> 00:32:29.621
And there, there is a concept in cognitive science

744
00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:32.163
called identity foreclosure.

745
00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:35.558
I highlighted that in my notes actually.

746
00:32:35.558 --> 00:32:37.870
'Cause I wanted to spend some time on that,

747
00:32:37.870 --> 00:32:41.556
this idea that we settle into, we can settle into

748
00:32:41.556 --> 00:32:45.570
a self identity early and close ourselves off from change.

749
00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:47.860
Got an early lesson in that.

750
00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:50.515
I absolutely felt prey to identity foreclosure,

751
00:32:50.515 --> 00:32:52.957
and didn't even know what it was at the time.

752
00:32:52.957 --> 00:32:55.125
First and foremost, a violinist.

753
00:32:55.125 --> 00:32:59.237
I saw that as my past, present and future.

754
00:32:59.237 --> 00:33:04.237
And I think it held me back feeling

755
00:33:04.312 --> 00:33:05.931
that my identity was so fixed.

756
00:33:05.931 --> 00:33:10.931
I would've benefited a lot from having a more malleable

757
00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:16.060
sense of self and attaching my identity to more stable

758
00:33:16.175 --> 00:33:18.700
features of things rather than the thing itself.

759
00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:19.570
That's the biggest lesson

760
00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:21.013
that I learned from all this, which is.

761
00:33:21.013 --> 00:33:22.677
But it's also very natural.

762
00:33:22.677 --> 00:33:25.910
We all do it to some extent I am this,

763
00:33:25.910 --> 00:33:26.908
I am my business card.

764
00:33:26.908 --> 00:33:29.351
I do this thing therefore I am that.

765
00:33:29.351 --> 00:33:32.623
Yeah, no, I completely agree and I think for me,

766
00:33:32.623 --> 00:33:35.615
what I learned over time and this hits on an,

767
00:33:35.615 --> 00:33:40.298
an earlier part of our conversation is

768
00:33:40.298 --> 00:33:44.317
what was it about the violin that really lit me up.

