01:34:36.590 --> 01:34:38.480
I guess on a definitional level is just

2305
01:34:38.480 --> 01:34:40.119
blending insights from psychology,

2306
01:34:40.119 --> 01:34:42.000
with insights, from economics

2307
01:34:42.000 --> 01:34:44.320
to a more robust understanding

2308
01:34:44.320 --> 01:34:46.330
of how humans operate in different settings.

2309
01:34:46.330 --> 01:34:47.920
I see the idea being that

2310
01:34:47.920 --> 01:34:49.506
we're not logical actors,

2311
01:34:49.506 --> 01:34:51.817
that we're prone to these emotional states

2312
01:34:51.817 --> 01:34:53.330
and these defaults that,

2313
01:34:53.330 --> 01:34:56.082
that lead us to make decisions that on paper,

2314
01:34:56.082 --> 01:34:57.966
it looks like we wouldn't otherwise.

2315
01:34:57.966 --> 01:34:59.775
Yeah and I mean,

2316
01:34:59.775 --> 01:35:02.076
there are some philosophical disagreements

2317
01:35:02.076 --> 01:35:03.243
about what is logical.

2318
01:35:03.243 --> 01:35:05.530
I mean, it is actually logical

2319
01:35:05.530 --> 01:35:07.920
sometimes for a human to act on their emotional states.

2320
01:35:07.920 --> 01:35:10.620
And so there's questions about that,

2321
01:35:10.620 --> 01:35:11.680
but I think that's exactly right.

2322
01:35:11.680 --> 01:35:14.238
It's that there's a lot of hidden,

2323
01:35:14.238 --> 01:35:16.532
surprising factors that our decisions

2324
01:35:16.532 --> 01:35:19.840
and our judgements outside of our conscious awareness.

2325
01:35:19.840 --> 01:35:22.500
I think one of the most illustrative examples of this

2326
01:35:22.500 --> 01:35:25.068
is in the context of voting.

2327
01:35:25.068 --> 01:35:26.360
We like to think that

2328
01:35:26.360 --> 01:35:27.840
we will just vote for the candidate.

2329
01:35:27.840 --> 01:35:29.268
We'd most like to see elected into office.

2330
01:35:29.268 --> 01:35:30.974
That feels pretty straightforward,

2331
01:35:30.974 --> 01:35:33.910
but there's research from Texas

2332
01:35:33.910 --> 01:35:36.330
showing that the order in which the candidate's

2333
01:35:36.330 --> 01:35:37.779
names appear ballot has an outsized

2334
01:35:37.779 --> 01:35:39.626
influence on voter behavior.

2335
01:35:39.626 --> 01:35:42.312
So if your, if your candidate's name is listed first,

2336
01:35:42.312 --> 01:35:44.470
that candidate will receive

2337
01:35:44.470 --> 01:35:46.930
a 10% point boost in vote share relative

2338
01:35:46.930 --> 01:35:48.178
to if they're listed last.

2339
01:35:48.178 --> 01:35:49.490
And obviously this is

2340
01:35:49.490 --> 01:35:50.970
in low information environments.

2341
01:35:50.970 --> 01:35:52.900
Of course, if you're dealing with a national election,

2342
01:35:52.900 --> 01:35:54.340
probably those ordering effects

2343
01:35:54.340 --> 01:35:56.640
are gonna be a lot less relevant or important.

2344
01:35:57.790 --> 01:36:01.136
But I feel like when we know that

2345
01:36:01.136 --> 01:36:02.940
when we know that we as humans

2346
01:36:02.940 --> 01:36:05.002
have this first in line bias,

2347
01:36:05.002 --> 01:36:06.730
it can lead us to design

2348
01:36:06.730 --> 01:36:09.240
more equitable solutions, right.

2349
01:36:09.240 --> 01:36:10.901
To design policy accordingly.

2350
01:36:10.901 --> 01:36:13.436
And what's happened is now that

2351
01:36:13.436 --> 01:36:15.090
now we randomize the order

2352
01:36:15.090 --> 01:36:17.280
in which Candidates appear in a lot of places

2353
01:36:17.280 --> 01:36:18.730
and a lot of election and context,

2354
01:36:18.730 --> 01:36:21.170
and that helps make things fair overall.

2355
01:36:21.170 --> 01:36:23.770
So the reason I'm so fascinated

2356
01:36:23.770 --> 01:36:25.990
by this field is that when we learn about it,

2357
01:36:25.990 --> 01:36:28.990
when we uncover insights in turn,

2358
01:36:28.990 --> 01:36:30.720
we can become smarter in correcting

2359
01:36:30.720 --> 01:36:33.670
for those biases solving for them

2360
01:36:33.670 --> 01:36:36.440
in the DNA of how we design policies and programs.

2361
01:36:36.440 --> 01:36:37.930
What are what are some of the initiatives

2362
01:36:37.930 --> 01:36:41.200
you would've like to have seen happen

2363
01:36:41.200 --> 01:36:42.260
when you were at the white house

2364
01:36:42.260 --> 01:36:44.320
that didn't happen or got nos,

2365
01:36:44.320 --> 01:36:46.602
but you feel like were good ideas.

2366
01:36:46.602 --> 01:36:47.680
That's a great question.

2367
01:36:47.680 --> 01:36:50.450
So one of the projects I was most passionate about,

2368
01:36:50.450 --> 01:36:52.773
about just came to a, an abrupt end.

2369
01:36:53.835 --> 01:36:55.750
My team, and I started working on the ground

2370
01:36:55.750 --> 01:36:56.730
in Flint, Michigan, and with,

2371
01:36:56.730 --> 01:36:57.810
with our colleagues there

2372
01:36:57.810 --> 01:36:59.620
during the lead and water crisis.

2373
01:36:59.620 --> 01:37:02.860
And initially we were working

2374
01:37:02.860 --> 01:37:05.142
in collaboration with white house offices

2375
01:37:05.142 --> 01:37:06.890
and also with the environmental

2376
01:37:06.890 --> 01:37:08.380
protection agency to make sure that

2377
01:37:08.380 --> 01:37:10.719
safe water information was getting

2378
01:37:10.719 --> 01:37:12.920
into the hands of Flint residents.

2379
01:37:12.920 --> 01:37:14.795
But then when we visited the ground

2380
01:37:14.795 --> 01:37:16.970
and we actually went there

2381
01:37:16.970 --> 01:37:18.972
and we talked to heads of churches and

2382
01:37:18.972 --> 01:37:21.007
heads of local, YMCAs,

2383
01:37:21.007 --> 01:37:23.689
and just people who were leading the troops,

2384
01:37:23.689 --> 01:37:28.689
we learned that the challenges

2385
01:37:28.755 --> 01:37:31.990
went so far back and that in, in many ways,

2386
01:37:31.990 --> 01:37:33.622
the lead water crisis was a symptom

2387
