01:42:50.290 --> 01:42:52.160
Yeah, one of my favorite insights

2524
01:42:52.160 --> 01:42:53.430
from behavioral science

2525
01:42:53.430 --> 01:42:54.750
in the area of trust building

2526
01:42:54.750 --> 01:42:57.246
is around what's called operational transparency.

2527
01:42:57.246 --> 01:42:59.594
And it refers to the idea that

2528
01:42:59.594 --> 01:43:01.268
when we pull the back

2529
01:43:01.268 --> 01:43:03.448
and we just let people in on

2530
01:43:03.448 --> 01:43:05.720
what's happening behind the scenes,

2531
01:43:05.720 --> 01:43:06.800
whether, in terms of what

2532
01:43:06.800 --> 01:43:08.880
constitutes our decision making process,

2533
01:43:08.880 --> 01:43:11.380
why we chose X over Y

2534
01:43:11.380 --> 01:43:13.160
the messiness behind the scenes.

2535
01:43:13.160 --> 01:43:14.360
I think my instinct as a leader

2536
01:43:14.360 --> 01:43:15.740
of my team is to protect people

2537
01:43:15.740 --> 01:43:17.120
from all the mess. It's like, oh,

2538
01:43:17.120 --> 01:43:18.120
my job is to protect you.

2539
01:43:18.120 --> 01:43:20.930
But actually it's kind of counterintuitive

2540
01:43:20.930 --> 01:43:22.160
to learn that back and be (indistinct).

2541
01:43:22.160 --> 01:43:23.660
Let me tell you about the mess.

2542
01:43:23.660 --> 01:43:25.580
Yeah, towards building trust

2543
01:43:25.580 --> 01:43:27.490
to shelter people too much from that process.

2544
01:43:27.490 --> 01:43:28.715
And so there was a really

2545
01:43:28.715 --> 01:43:33.110
interesting study run by some

2546
01:43:33.110 --> 01:43:35.530
Harvard psychologists with the city of Boston,

2547
01:43:35.530 --> 01:43:37.300
in which people were complaining

2548
01:43:37.300 --> 01:43:39.540
